    "created_at": "2025-04-01T00:36:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-01T00:36:22Z",
    "body": "> Firefox currently doesn't do that because of concerns about memory usage. I don't think adding a token would help as attackers would set that token. We need to figure out a strategy that doesn't make it overtly expensive. One thing I discussed with [@mystor](https://github.com/mystor) is that we'd at most use 2x the amount of processes. E.g., you'd have a process for origin A, and you'd have a process for \"origin\" A, sandboxed (this is technically an opaque origin, but for process isolation purposes you'd keep the initial origin around).\n> \n> That's still not ideal of course, but it's an improvement and hopefully over time we can do more work to make processes cheaper (or wait long enough for magical hardware to be there).\n\nThis has now been implemented in Chromium, as shown by https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40082497 being fixed.  Has it been implemented in Firefox?\n\nThe current situation essentially forecloses IoT devices (and others where simplicity of deployment on a LAN is critical) from implementing any form of sandboxed client-side plugins.  The reason is that the UI of such devices can generally only be displayed on a single domain, as implementing subdomains requires a more complex DNS configuration that might not be available.  Using `/etc/hosts` for these devices not uncommon, at least in my experience.\n\nWhat these devices really want is to be able to have arbitrarily many isolated origins (and thus isolated _processes_, so that `SharedArrayWorker` can be used) from a _single_ DNS domain.  `Content-Security-Policy: sandbox` would be used to ensure that the less-trusted content is cross-origin from a browser perspective.",
